{
  "about": "Frozen parity fixture for the vendored PathoROB split-mapping helper. Each case is the training-row schedule PathoROB's own get_patches_map_to_split produces for one of its three downstream datasets, split by split. croma.downstream.pathorob_schedule must reproduce it exactly. Read by tests/test_downstream_probe.py; the vendoring itself is governed by docs/adr/0011-downstream-shortcut-susceptibility-ships-in-the-library.md.",
  "rule": "The schedule is what makes a sweep 'confounder-biased': it is the sequence of training compositions whose accuracies APD reduces, so a drifted schedule redefines the metric while every value still computes. Nothing here may be regenerated from croma's copy -- capture it by executing upstream's function in a clean upstream checkout, and only as part of a deliberate re-vendor, never to make a red test green. It is hermetic on purpose: no PathoROB checkout, no PATHOROB_ROOT and no conditional skip, because CI is the branch that gates merges and a test that skips there guards nothing.",
  "source": {
    "project": "PathoROB",
    "url": "https://github.com/bifold-pathomics/PathoROB",
    "revision": "6583cf0b0d902c8cc032308262fa3a3befdc0687",
    "helper": "pathorob/apd/utils.py :: get_patches_map_to_split",
    "num_splits_from": "pathorob/apd/utils.py :: load_data, which pairs each dataset with the number of splits and the patches-per-slide the PathoROB paper reports for it (camelyon 8/300, tcga 7/30, tolkach_esca 4/100)"
  },
  "procedure": [
    "Clone https://github.com/bifold-pathomics/PathoROB at the revision recorded above.",
    "From that checkout, import pathorob.apd.utils.get_patches_map_to_split -- no features, model weights, GPU or croma import.",
    "For each case below, call it for every split in range(num_splits) with the recorded num_patches_per_slide, and record (split_map, max_train_slides) as returned.",
    "Each case is captured twice: once at the dataset's published patches-per-slide, and once at 1, which is what a slide-level sweep passes.",
    "Write the result out with json.dump(..., indent=2)."
  ],
